---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { repoUrl, siteConfig } from '../lib/site/config';
import { url } from '../lib/site/url';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="What this site is, who it is for, and how it is built.">
  <div class="prose page" data-pagefind-body>
    <h1>About</h1>
    <h2>What this is</h2>
    <p>
      {siteConfig.title} is a free series of posts on state estimation. It starts with a single Gaussian and works up to
      the sparse optimization problems at the heart of modern SLAM (simultaneous localization and mapping). Along the
      way it covers least squares, the Kalman filter and its nonlinear relatives, particle filters, rotations and Lie
      groups, factor graphs and pose-graph optimization.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each post pairs a written explanation with interactive figures that run in your browser. You can drag a covariance
      ellipse, tune a filter’s noise model or break an optimizer and see what happens. Nothing is a pre-recorded video.
    </p>
    <h2>Who it is for</h2>
    <p>
      Engineering students, robotics and autonomy engineers, and anyone learning on their own. The series assumes
      undergraduate calculus, basic linear algebra (matrix multiplication, inverses, eigenvalues) and basic probability.
      Everything else is introduced as it is needed. The <a href={`${url('')}#syllabus`}>syllabus</a> lists the posts in
      order.
    </p>
    <h2>How it is built</h2>
    <p>
      The site is static, built with Astro and hosted on GitHub Pages. The interactive figures are Svelte components;
      the mathematics behind them lives in a small, unit-tested TypeScript library, so the numbers you see in a figure
      come from the same code that the tests check. Equations are typeset with KaTeX.
    </p>
    <p>
      The code and the text are written with Claude Code, Anthropic’s AI coding assistant, under the direction and
      review of the site’s owner. Every post goes through automated checks before it is published: unit tests for the
      mathematics, including identities such as “the Kalman filter equals batch least squares”, a script that checks
      each claim the text makes about a figure against that figure’s default settings, and browser tests on phone and
      desktop widths. If you find an error, please <a href={`${repoUrl}/issues/new`}>open an issue</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Licenses</h2>
    <p>
      Text and figures are licensed under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. The code
      is under the <a href={`${repoUrl}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>MIT license</a>. The source is on
      <a href={repoUrl}>GitHub</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Privacy</h2>
    <p>The site sets no cookies and uses no analytics. Your theme choice is stored only in your own browser.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page {
    padding: var(--space-5) var(--gutter);
    max-width: calc(var(--w-text) + 2 * var(--gutter));
  }
</style>
